{"id":"7b32fe3e-05a5-4186-aac1-ab1536ed95bb","object":"chat.completion","created":1793336510,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ calls>\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ invoke name=\"update_plan\">\n<｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter name=\"items\" string=\"false\">[{\"title\": \"Read required source files\", \"status\": \"done\"}, {\"title\": \"Run baseline pytest\", \"status\": \"done\"}, {\"title\": \"Implement environ.py config\", \"status\": \"doing\"}, {\"title\": \"Implement logger.py formatter budget\", \"status\": \"todo\"}, {\"title\": \"Implement stats_logger.py extra flag\", \"status\": \"todo\"}, {\"title\": \"Run final pytest and produce patch/logs\", \"status\": \"todo\"}]</｜｜DSML｜｜ parameter>\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ invoke>\n</｜｜DSML｜｜ calls>","reasoning_content":"I have all the mechanisms exposed. Now I need to implement. Let me plan the edits.\n\nPlan:\n1. environ.py: add factory method `int_or_none_min` or a lambdas. Requirement: attribute DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS, None if unset, else int >=32, error message must contain variable name. The EnvVar descriptor wraps errors with f\"{self.name}: {e}\", so name is included automatically. But if we use a lambda that raises ValueError, it will be wrapped. Good. Need empty string -> ValueError (int(\"\") raises ValueError). Non-parsable -> ValueError from int(). But be careful: int() raises TypeError for None handled. Only ValueError wrapping. Actually int(None) would raise TypeError, but we only call int when v is not None.\n\nSo define helper:\n\n```python\ndef _min_int_or_none(v: str | None, minimum: int = 32) -> int | None:\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    value = int(v)\n    if value < minimum:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{value} < {minimum}\")\n    return value\n```\n\nThe error message after wrapping: \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: ...\" contains variable name. Good.\n\nAdd factory method `int_or_none_with_min`? Simpler: use `EnvVar(lambda v: _min_int_or_none(v))` in class. The task says \"描述符类可以新增辅助工厂方法\". Let's add a factory method for clarity:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int = 0) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: _min_int_or_none(v, minimum))\n```\n\nThen in envs under Logging: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)`.\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only `max_message_chars: int | None = None`. Validate: if not None and (isinstance(bool) or not isinstance int or <32) raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation of truncation in format(): need to first do arg substitution, then truncate the message body, without mutating shared record. Approach: \n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        # need original message after % substitution, without altering record\n        ...\n```\n\nThe cleanest: create a copy of the record using `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)`? But copying may drop custom attributes? Actually makeLogRecord(dict) creates a new LogRecord with the dict updated. But record.message may be set. Simpler: temporarily compute the message ourselves and override.\n\nOption: compute message via `record.getMessage()`. Then temporarily set record.msg = truncated message, record.args = () during super().format(record), then restore in finally. Requirement says \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\" — this means final state must not be altered; temporary override with restore is arguably borderline. Safer: copy the record.\n\nApproach:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if (self.max_message_chars is not None\n            and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True):\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n        if truncated != message:\n            record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = None  # getMessage returns str(msg) if no args\n            record.message = truncated\n            record.exc_text = None  # ensure recomputation? \n```\n\nHmm, exc_info kept in copy dict, fine. But `record.message` attribute already set? Actually record.message only set when Formatter.format runs. If format called twice on same record (two handlers), first handler sets record.message on the original record (logging.Formatter.format sets `record.message = record.getMessage()`), which is fine; after our copy, we set message explicitly.\n\nFor multi-line handling, the existing code uses record.message. In format, `msg = super().format(record)` uses record.message after substitution internally. Then if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message: prefix computed by find(record.message). If we copied record and set record.message = truncated, that works.\n\nBut careful: exc_info formatting. logging.Formatter.format uses record.exc_info and appends traceback; our budget shouldn't include it. Since the truncated message ends before traceback appended, fine. Provide exc_text? If cached exc_text from a previous handler formatting... logging's format uses `self.formatException(record.exc_info)` if record.exc_text is None... Actually code:\n\n```python\nif record.exc_info:\n    if not record.exc_text:\n        record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)\nif record.exc_text:\n    ...\n```\n\nOn the copy, exc_text copied from original dict — includes traceback text; unchanged from what would be produced. Fine.\n\nAlso stack_info: uses record.stack_info directly.\n\nNow `record.args = None` - getMessage: `if self.args: msg = msg % self.args`. So set args to None good. Or `()`.\n\nAlso set `record.message = truncated` maybe not required since super().format sets it.\n\nTruncate function:\n\n```python\n_OMISSION = \"...<omitted>...\"\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    marker = _OMISSION\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message[:max_chars]  # shouldn't happen since max_chars>=32 > len(marker)=14\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nCheck budget 32: remaining = 32-15? marker \"...<omitted>...\" length: 3 dots + \"<omitted>\" 9 chars? \"<omitted>\" = 1+7+1 = 9, plus 3+3 = 15. remaining = 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches example (9 head, 8 tail, 15 marker). \n\nDefault init in _setup_logger: `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Need import envs in logger.py. Careful about circular imports: does environ import logger? environ imports os, threading, datetime. No circular. But logger imports envs — check environ doesn't import logger. It doesn't (only shown top imports). Good.\n\nBut wait, `from deepinfer import environ` maybe use `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Check whether other modules import pattern. stats_logger imports `from deepinfer.logger import init_logger`. Let's use `from deepinfer.environ import envs`.\n\nCareful: accessing envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS at import time `_setup_logger()` triggers parse and cache — that's intended (\"首次访问配置时读取环境变量\"). But if env var invalid, logger import fails with ValueError. Tests probably set var then import. Hmm, but potential issue: default module import `_setup_logger()` runs at import; if the env var is set to invalid value, import raises ValueError — that's expected behavior probably.\n\nHowever a concern: cached value at first access. If test does `envs.rearm(...)` then `_setup_logger()`, our init reads config value → new budget. Good.\n\n3. stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nLet me also ensure validation for `max_message_chars`: bool not allowed.\n\n```python\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n```\n\nTask says ValueError for invalid; type check message content not specified strictness.\n\nAlso \"保留原有四个位置参数，新参数 max_message_chars 只能按名称传入\" → keyword-only: `*, max_message_chars: int | None = None`.\n\nNow let's write edits. Let me check exact text of enums etc. I have environ lines 1-30, 31-94, 95-221 full file likely. Need full envs section text for replace. Let's do replacements.\n\nFirst environ.py: add helper function after `_str` and factory method after int_or_none, plus class attribute.\n\nLet me get file content to check the exact ending and whether `envs` class has more. Already seen lines 95-221 through rearm_all. Total file maybe 221 lines.\n\nEdits to environ.py:\n1. Add `_int_or_none_min` helper after `_str`.\n2. Add static factory method `int_or_none_min` after `int_or_none`.\n3. Add `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)` in Logging section.\n\nAlso note _descriptors uses name. Fine.\n\nLet me write."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10238,"completion_tokens":2178,"total_tokens":12416,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2040},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":6142},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}